en.help.message:
Deno's language server provides completion, diagnostics, hover, navigation and formatting for TypeScript and JavaScript.

Install the Deno runtime first:
  curl -fsSL https://deno.land/install.sh | sh
  brew install deno

A Deno project needs deno.json or deno.jsonc at its root. gvim cannot switch a language server automatically from a plugin, so add deno lsp to the TypeScript and JavaScript entries in config.json (and restart the server after installing):

  "lsp": {
    "typescript": {
      "command": "deno", "args": ["lsp"], "auto_start": false,
      "root_markers": ["deno.json", "deno.jsonc"],
      "initialization_options": { "enable": true }
    },
    "javascript": {
      "command": "deno", "args": ["lsp"], "auto_start": false,
      "root_markers": ["deno.json", "deno.jsonc"],
      "initialization_options": { "enable": true }
    }
  }

Notes:
  * Deno: Check Project reports the project config and the deno lookup on PATH.
  * The PATH lookup is local; in a remote workspace the check describes the local machine, while the configuration still applies to the workspace.
  * Deno LSP is started on demand: open a TypeScript/JavaScript file and use Start/Restart LSP Server.

Docs: https://docs.deno.com/runtime/getting_started/installation/
